// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`bundled piggyback fallback classifier template > matches the recorded snapshot 1`] = `
{
  "system": "# Calendar
This story tracks time in days (86400 seconds per day). Tiers: month (Frostmoon and Thawmoon). Convert relative-time prose ("two days later", "the next morning") into a seconds delta using these units.

# The user's turns
A turn marked as the user's is what the lead does, says or attempts. Where the narration after it shows how an attempt turned out, the narration decides what happened, and what the lead says stands unless the narration contradicts it. A user's turn that plainly speaks to the narrator rather than playing the lead ("skip to the next morning") is a direction: it states nothing that happened, so take the facts from the narration.",
  "user": "Known entities, referenced only by the ID shown below in brackets — write it without the brackets, never invent one:
- [c1] Kael (character)
- [l1] The Drowned Keep (location)
- [i3] Courier’s seal (item)
- [i4] short sword (item)
- [i5] a rusted lantern (item)
- [i9] Veilstone (item, staged)

Everything from here to "# This turn" is the world as it stands going into that turn — the baseline your report starts from, not a record of anything that happened in it.
Take the changes from the turn below; nothing above it happened this turn.

# In scene

## Kael [c1]
A courier with a stolen sigil.
Holds:
- [i3] Courier’s seal: Black wax, cracked.
- [i4] short sword (equipped)

# Current location

[l1] The Drowned Keep — in The Marsh: Half sunk into the marsh.
Here:
- [i5] a rusted lantern

# Places
[l2] The Marsh
  [l1] The Drowned Keep
Unplaced:
  [l9] Varn

# The wider world

[c7] Mora (not in the scene): last seen at the ford.

# Relevant lore

House Eldrin
An exiled line.

# What has happened

The siege broke on the third night.

# Active threads

Find the heir
Still open.

# Earlier chapters

Chapter One: the marsh road.

# Named in this turn — not necessarily present

[c8] Ilse: A ferrywoman.
Voice: soft

# Earlier turns — how the scene got here, not the turn you report on

The marsh road ended at a wall of black water.

Kael went first, testing the stones.

# This turn

I put the sword away and raised both hands.

The keep answered with a single lantern, swinging.

Report the scene state as of the LAST entry above, the time delta, a one-sentence summary of the turn, up to three retrieval queries naming context you want looked up for the next turn, and anything else the schema asks for that this turn showed.
Scene state is absolute, not a delta: report the full cast present at the end of the turn, not only what changed. Read the entry before it for state the user's own action changed.
For the time delta, give seconds elapsed since the previous entry, including any time the user's action itself took (0 for a flashback or memory; never negative).

Report a place the scene settled in that has no ID yet in newLocations, and an item someone took or was given that has no ID yet in newItems. A handle is the one reference you make up: give each a handle that starts with new: (e.g. new:docks), and use it like an ID — a new place as currentLocation or as another new place's parent, a new item as a transfer's item id or, when it is in the scene, in sceneEntities.

Create a place only when the scene settles somewhere with its own identity the story could come back to: a named place, a building, a room that matters, a stretch of wild land where things happen. Don't create one for passing through (a corridor, a doorway, the road between two places); keep the current location then. Use an existing place's ID whenever the scene is somewhere already in Places, and nest a new place under the place it's part of.

Set a new place's parent to top only when nothing in Places could contain it, as for the story's first place or a city when Places holds only cities. Leave its parent out when something in Places could contain it and the prose doesn't say which; when unsure, leave it out.

Create an item only when a character takes, receives or is given something that matters beyond this moment: a weapon, a key, a letter, a relic. Not food eaten on the spot, not money (use a stackable), not scenery.

Give an existing place or item a condition only when the prose changes it, and only one shown above with its state: the current location, an item held in the scene or lying there, a place or item listed elsewhere in the world. Write it as it is now, the running consequence rather than the event (burned to the ground, not the fire), in a few words, never more than about 60; it replaces the stored condition whole, so never restate one unchanged. A place or item you create takes its condition where you create it.

Put an item down only when someone holding it sets it down where the scene is and it is still lying there when the turn ends, so an item picked up again isn't put down; an item put down anywhere else, thrown into a river or left in another place, stays prose.
",
}
`;
